{"document":null,"evidence_ids":["P1:e1","P1:e2","P1:e3","P1:e4"],"paper":{"abstract":"Abstract Autonomous Large Language Model (LLM) agents have demonstrated significant capability in complex problem-solving. However, existing multi-agent systems predominantly rely on fixed, static execution topologies where a predefined suite of specialist agents is invoked sequentially regardless of query complexity. This rigid paradigm imposes a heavy computational tax, leading to inflated token consumption, high execution latency, and elevated operational costs on simple to moderately complex tasks. In this paper, we propose TCA-DARE (Task-Complexity-Aware Dynamic Agent Routing and Execution), an adaptive multi-agent framework that evaluates incoming academic requests prior to agent instantiation and allocates a minimal viable agent topology. By combining a lightweight four-feature mathematical complexity-scoring router (Layer 1), a dynamic specialist agent pool (Layer 2), a source-grounding verification guardrail (Layer 3, specified for future integration), and a telemetry logger (Layer 4), the framework routes queries across three distinct execution topologies: single-agent extraction (C₁), dual-pipeline synthesis (C₂), and full multi-agent orchestration (C₃). We present the complete system architecture, formal routing algorithm, and an implemented (not yet experimentally executed) local Python prototype built on Ollama (llama3) and tiktoken, alongside a structured multi-tier evaluation protocol designed for upcoming empirical validation against static multi-agent baselines.","arxiv_id":null,"authors":[{"name":"Moksha Arora"},{"name":"Bhawna Mittal"},{"name":"Prof. Mohit Tiwari"}],"doi":"10.21203/rs.3.rs-11814023/v1","paper_id":"P1","title":"TCA-DARE: A Task-Complexity-Aware Dynamic Agent Routing Framework for Multi-Agent LLM Systems","url":"https://doi.org/10.21203/rs.3.rs-11814023/v1","venue":null,"year":2026},"record":{"alternatives":[],"message":"Exact identifier verified